s on a proposed settlement of Administrative Civil Liability Complaint No. ... R4-2024-0016\n\nImage: Enforcement Program Logo\n\n# Enforcement\n\nThe Porter-Cologne Water Quality Control Act gives the Water Boards the authority to protect groundwater and surface waters of the State. The State Board's Water Quality Enforcement Policy (Policy) helps define an enforcement process to efficiently address water quality problems. The Enforcement Program ensures that dischargers comply with their permits so the Water Board can meet its water quality goals. Enforcement staff do this by implementing the Policy and by reviewing monitoring reports, inspecting sites, and taking follow up enforcement as needed for the Region's NPDES and Sanitary Sewer System Permittees. Enforcement staff also manage the SEP Program, the Complaint Triage Program, the AGO Pilot Project, the Cross Programs Pilot Project, and the CIWQS and ESMR Programs.\n\n  * What's New!\n  * Enforcement Actions\n  * Complaints\n  * Current Cases\n  * SEPs\n\n  * cite6†Public Notice No. 25-011 - Notice of opportunity for public comment regarding proposed settlement involving The Boeing Company Santa Susana Field Laboratory. Comment Deadline: 5:00 pm on January 15, 2026: The Los Angeles Regional Water Quality Control Board is accepting public comments on a proposed settlement of Administrative Civil Liability Complaint No. R4-2025-0268. Please click here for further information and instructions on how to submit comments. * Administrative Civil Liability Complaint (ACLC) No. R4-2025-0268\n    * Attachments A, B, and C to ACLC No. R4-2025-0268\n    * Signed Waiver Form\n\n  * Los Angeles Water Board introduces expedited enforcement of municipal stormwater violations\n    * City of Los Angeles, Los Angeles County Flood Control District, Los Angeles County, City of Lomita, City of Carson, City of Torrance, City of Rolling Hills, City of Rolling Hills Estates, City of Palos Verdes Estates, City of Rancho Palos Verdes